piscsi for Debian
=================

The supplied systemd units are enabled and started by package installation.
This package set is intended for Raspberry Pi systems with PiSCSI hardware.
Stop or disable a service explicitly if its optional hardware is not attached.

The web interface reads its protected configuration from
/etc/piscsi-web/piscsi-web.env. Its session key is generated on first package
configuration and is retained on upgrades and removal. Do not delete it unless
you deliberately want to invalidate existing browser sessions.
Its editable drive-property presets are in
/etc/piscsi-web/drive_properties.json.

The OLED service needs an I2C device whose permissions allow the i2c group to
open it. The Control Board service runs as root because it needs the GPIO
character device as well as I2C.

All shared image, upload, and configuration directories are managed by
systemd-tmpfiles. They are intentionally retained when packages are removed.

Debian Trixie cross-builds
--------------------------

The top-level `debian/` directory builds four binary packages from one source
tree: `piscsi`, `piscsi-web`, `piscsi-oled`, and `piscsi-ctrlboard`. Keeping one
source package is necessary because the C++ daemon, the Go services, and their
generated Protocol Buffer bindings share `proto/piscsi_interface.proto`.

`piscsi` is built with the classic `cpp/Makefile`. The remaining packages are
pure-Go (`CGO_ENABLED=0`) and need no target C compiler.

One-time Trixie build-host setup
--------------------------------

Trixie supplies Go 1.24, but this checkout's `go.mod` requires Go 1.25 and
selects `go1.25.12`. For a local build, install a Go 1.25.12-or-newer toolchain
outside the package build. The following installs Go 1.25.13 for an arm64
build host without replacing Trixie's `/usr/bin/go`. Use the archive for the
build host architecture, not the package target architecture.

```sh
cd /tmp
curl -fLO https://go.dev/dl/go1.25.13.linux-arm64.tar.gz
echo 'adad240fcb6bd180cf973b4b7c747baf4ec81d08b7d40ca35940ee4531971490  go1.25.13.linux-arm64.tar.gz' | sha256sum -c -
sudo install -d /opt/go1.25.13
sudo tar -C /opt/go1.25.13 --strip-components=1 \
  -xzf go1.25.13.linux-arm64.tar.gz
```

Set the local toolchain explicitly. `GOTOOLCHAIN=local` prevents Go from
downloading a toolchain during the package build.

The Go module must be vendored before the source package is made; Debian build
environments must not fetch modules from the network. Generate the local Go
Protocol Buffer bindings first: without them, `go mod vendor` mistakes the
local `go/proto` package for a missing remote dependency.

```sh
export PATH=/opt/go1.25.13/bin:$PATH
export GOTOOLCHAIN=local
go version
go env GOVERSION | awk -F. '($1 == "go1" && $2 >= 25) { ok = 1 } END { exit !ok }'
(cd go && ./proto/generate.sh && go mod vendor && cd ..)
```

The generated binding is removed by `debian/rules clean` and recreated during
the build; this is expected. Keep the generated `go/vendor/` directory in the
upstream source tarball.

Install the native build tools and, for each target, its cross toolchain and
target development libraries. `protoc` and `protoc-gen-go` run on the build
machine, so do not install target-architecture versions of those tools.

```sh
sudo apt update
sudo apt install --yes devscripts equivs fakeroot \
  debhelper build-essential pkgconf protobuf-compiler protoc-gen-go \
  golang-go libfmt-dev libgmock-dev libgtest-dev libpcap-dev libprotobuf-dev \
  libspdlog-dev
```

Development test build
----------------------

Build and run the full test suite on your development machine.:

```sh
debuild --prepend-path=/opt/go1.25.13/bin -b -us -uc
```

`debuild` resets `PATH` by default, so an ordinary `PATH=... debuild` command
will still select Trixie's Go 1.24. `--prepend-path` retains the normal Debian
sanitised environment while making the local Go toolchain available. This
approach is suitable for local development builds only: an official clean
Debian build needs a satisfiable Debian build dependency for Go 1.25 or newer.

The resulting `.deb` files are written to the parent directory. Installation
enables and starts every supplied service, which is the expected behaviour for
the Raspberry Pi appliance image.

arm64
-----

```sh
sudo dpkg --add-architecture arm64
sudo apt update
sudo apt install --yes crossbuild-essential-arm64 \
  libfmt-dev:arm64 libpcap-dev:arm64 libprotobuf-dev:arm64 libspdlog-dev:arm64
dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b -aarm64
```

The C++ rule expands the Debian host triplet to
`CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu-`; Go receives `GOARCH=arm64`.

armhf
-----

```sh
sudo dpkg --add-architecture armhf
sudo apt update
sudo apt install --yes crossbuild-essential-armhf \
  libfmt-dev:armhf libpcap-dev:armhf libprotobuf-dev:armhf libspdlog-dev:armhf
dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b -aarmhf
```

The C++ rule expands the Debian host triplet to
`CROSS_COMPILE=arm-linux-gnueabihf-`; Go receives `GOARCH=arm GOARM=7`, which
is Debian's armhf baseline.

Raspberry Pi Zero
-----------------

Raspberry Pi OS reports the original Raspberry Pi Zero as `armhf`, but its
ARMv6 hard-float ABI is different from Debian's ARMv7 armhf baseline. Build in
an ARMv6 Raspberry Pi OS/Raspbian armhf rootfs; do not use Debian or Ubuntu
armhf target libraries, as they may contain ARMv7 instructions.

The historical `piscsi-pi-gen` project provides a proven rootfs setup: it
bootstraps an armhf Raspbian rootfs and configures the Raspbian package
repository. On a Linux development host, use that rootfs under QEMU user-mode
emulation; on macOS, run the same setup in a privileged Linux VM or container.

On a Linux development host, create the Raspbian Trixie rootfs as follows. The
Raspbian archive key is used by debootstrap to verify the downloaded packages.

```sh
sudo apt update
sudo apt install --yes debootstrap qemu-user-static binfmt-support curl gpg rsync

ROOTFS=/srv/piscsi-rpi-zero-trixie
sudo install -d "$ROOTFS" /usr/share/keyrings
curl -fsSL https://archive.raspbian.org/raspbian.public.key | \
  sudo gpg --dearmor --yes -o /usr/share/keyrings/raspbian-archive-keyring.gpg
sudo debootstrap --arch=armhf --foreign \
  --components=main,contrib,non-free \
  --keyring=/usr/share/keyrings/raspbian-archive-keyring.gpg \
  trixie "$ROOTFS" http://raspbian.raspberrypi.com/raspbian/
sudo install -m 0755 "$(command -v qemu-arm-static)" "$ROOTFS/usr/bin/qemu-arm-static"
sudo chroot "$ROOTFS" /debootstrap/debootstrap --second-stage
sudo tee "$ROOTFS/etc/apt/sources.list" >/dev/null <<'EOF'
deb http://raspbian.raspberrypi.org/raspbian/ trixie main contrib non-free rpi
EOF
```

Mount the kernel interfaces needed by APT and copy the source tree into the
rootfs. Copying rather than bind-mounting prevents the emulated root build from
leaving root-owned files in the development checkout.

```sh
sudo mount --bind /dev "$ROOTFS/dev"
sudo mount --bind /dev/pts "$ROOTFS/dev/pts"
sudo mount -t proc proc "$ROOTFS/proc"
sudo mount -t sysfs sys "$ROOTFS/sys"
sudo install -d "$ROOTFS/build/piscsi"
sudo rsync -a --delete --exclude .git ./ "$ROOTFS/build/piscsi/"
sudo chroot "$ROOTFS" /bin/bash
```

For subsequent builds, leave the chroot, update the host checkout as usual,
then rerun the `rsync` command before entering the chroot again. The host
checkout is the source of truth: `--delete` removes stale files from the
rootfs copy, and excluding `.git` is intentional, so do not run Git commands
inside the rootfs copy.

Inside the resulting ARMv6 Raspbian shell, install the build dependencies and
the official Go 1.25.13 ARMv6 (`linux-armv6l`) toolchain, then build:

```sh
export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt update
apt install --yes devscripts equivs fakeroot debhelper build-essential pkgconf \
  protobuf-compiler protoc-gen-go golang-go libfmt-dev libgmock-dev libgtest-dev \
  libpcap-dev libprotobuf-dev libspdlog-dev ca-certificates curl
cd /tmp
curl -fLO https://go.dev/dl/go1.25.13.linux-armv6l.tar.gz
install -d /opt/go1.25.13
tar -C /opt/go1.25.13 --strip-components=1 \
  -xzf go1.25.13.linux-armv6l.tar.gz
cd /build/piscsi

export PATH=/opt/go1.25.13/bin:$PATH
export GOTOOLCHAIN=local
DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck PISCSI_RPI_ZERO=1 dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b
```

`PISCSI_RPI_ZERO=1` keeps the resulting package architecture as `armhf` while
using `-march=armv6 -mfpu=vfp -mfloat-abi=hard` for C++ and `GOARM=6` for the
Go binaries. The flag is deliberately opt-in, so ordinary Debian armhf builds
continue to target ARMv7. `DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck` skips the C++ and Go test
suites, which are especially slow under ARM emulation; omit it when you want
to run the tests.

Leave the chroot with `exit`. The generated packages are in `$ROOTFS/build/`,
the parent directory of the copied source tree, and the rootfs is directly
accessible from the host. Copy them to a host directory with:

```sh
mkdir -p ./rpi-zero-packages
sudo rsync -a --chown="$(id -u):$(id -g)" \
  --include='*_armhf.deb' --exclude='*' \
  "$ROOTFS/build/" ./rpi-zero-packages/
```

Then unmount the rootfs in reverse order:

```sh
sudo umount "$ROOTFS/sys"
sudo umount "$ROOTFS/proc"
sudo umount "$ROOTFS/dev/pts"
sudo umount "$ROOTFS/dev"
```
